  "GSE350838": {
   "record": {
    "accession": "GSE350838",
    "title": "Evaluating the toxicity and efficacy of the YAP inhibitor verteporfin in combination with pazopanib in a synovial sarcoma xenograft model (UZLX-STS7)",
    "summary": "Nuclear Yes-associated protein1 (YAP1) is expressed at higher levels in synovial sarcoma, myxoid liposarcoma, and malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors than in other soft tissue sarcomas. To further characterize YAP1 activity, we had used RNA sequencing from 45 synovial sarcoma patient samples.15 samples with high YAP1 pathway activity scores had enriched gene sets that related to vasculature, including FGF2. Pazopanib is currently used as a single-agent therapy for synovial sarcoma and targets growth factor receptors. Therefore, we investigated the potential relationship between YAP1 and pazopanib. This data is from the in vivo experiment that evaluated the toxicity and efficacy of the YAP inhibitor verteporfin in combination with pazopanib in a synovial sarcoma xenograft model (UZLX-STS7).",
    "overall_design": "An efficacy study  was performed to evaluate the efficacy of the combination treatment with pazopanib and verteporfin in NMRI nu/nu mice transplanted bilaterally with the SynSa patient-derived xenograft model UZLX-STS7. This model retains stable histopathological and molecular features similar to synovial sarcoma patients. Animals were randomized into treatment groups (7-8 mice/group) on day 1 and treated for 23 days with vehicle, pazopanib, verteporfin, or a combination of pazopanib and verteporfin. Pazopanib was purchased from Carbosynth (Compton, UK) and prepared as 10 mg/ml solution in 1% hydroxypropylmethylcellulose and 0.1% Tween 80 (vehicle). Verteporfin was purchased from Adooq Bioscience (California, USA) and dissolved in 10% dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO) to 100 mg/ml concentration as a stock solution; before administration it was diluted 10X with aqua ad injectabilia (10 mg/ml). Pazopanib at a dose of 100 mg/kg once daily by oral gavage; verteporfin was given at 75 mg/kg every two days by intraperitoneal (i.p.) injection. Dosing was based on previous literature, where the drugs were used either as monotherapy or in combination with other agents, such as doxorubicin. Two mice from each treatment group were evaluated with RNA sequencing.",
